  <h2>What is adjustability?</h2>

  <p>
    Everything you have learned so far remains the same.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your load.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your balance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your firing sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your barrel path.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing changes.
  </p>

  <p class="emphasis">
    The swing doesn't change.
    <br />
    The pitch does.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every load should prepare the hitter to handle the fastest pitch that
    pitcher can throw.
  </p>

  <p>
    From the moment the ball is released, the hitter begins gathering as much
    information as possible.
  </p>

  <p>
    Speed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Location.
  </p>

  <p>
    Changes in elevation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Spin.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every fraction of a second provides another piece of information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once enough information has been processed, two things ultimately dictate
    the swing.
  </p>

  <p class="emphasis">
    Speed determines when the swing begins.
    <br />
    Location determines where contact occurs.
  </p>

  <p>
    An ideal pitch down the middle is typically contacted about one baseball
    inside or outside the front foot.
  </p>

  <p>
    An inside pitch moves the contact point farther out in front, generally one
    to three baseballs in front of the front foot.
  </p>

  <p>
    An outside pitch moves the contact point farther back, generally one to
    three baseballs toward the center of the body.
  </p>

  <p>
    The load remains the same.
  </p>

  <p>
    The power transfer remains the same.
  </p>

  <p>
    The barrel is still delivered through an efficient sequence.
  </p>

  <p class="final-thought">
    The swing hasn't changed.
    <br />
    The contact point has.
  </p>
</ChapterLayout>